tificial intelligence (AI) in digital pathology offers opportunities for improving diagnostic process speed, accuracy, and scalability. The availability of well-structured, annotated histopathology datasets is essential to this advancement. This study provides an extensive overview of publicly available datasets tailored specifically for histopathology-related AI and machine learning research. Our review yielded 151 datasets across tissue types and cancers (gastrointestinal, brain glioma, lung adenocarcinoma, and others). We also categorize the datasets in terms of the number of patients, organs, staining, magnification, scanner, size, collected method, year, and resolution. We analyze multiple key and popular datasets, including but not limited to CAMELYON, TUPAC, MIDOG, MoNuSeg, and BreakHis. We believe this review will help computational histopathology research by providing a comprehensive understanding of the available datasets, their structures, and their specific applications. Researchers can more effectively choose relevant datasets for creating AI models suited to certain tasks, such as cancer diagnosis, treatment response prediction, and tissue classification, by documenting and evaluating these resources. Within the field, standardizing images across these datasets can facilitate collaborations between the data-generating experts, pathologists, and AI model developers, as well as help in reproducibility, benchmark testing, and evaluation. Furthermore, by combining histopathological, radiological, and genomic data, for example, this evaluation will help identify gaps in the availability of current datasets. Another benefit is that it will help identify the need for additional diversified datasets that incorporate multimodal data. Closing these gaps will be essential to creating AI models that are applicable to different types of institutions and patient groups.","doi":"10.1007/s10278-026-01899-y","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000280","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a systematic review of publicly available histopathology datasets for AI research, so it is a resource/benchmark paper rather than a candidate model paper. No text component is described.","evidence_snippet":"“systematic review” of “publicly available datasets tailored specifically for histopathology-related AI”"},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000280","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a systematic review of public pathology datasets for AI, not a primary model paper. It catalogs datasets and applications rather than presenting a generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematic review\" of \"publicly available datasets\" for histopathology-related AI"},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"benchmark_resource","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"no"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000281","title":"Integrating host-microbiome multi-omics with machine learning: methods, benchmarks, and translational applications","abstract":"… complexity across genomic, transcriptomic, proteomic, and … Collectively, LLM-based approaches are rapidly advancing … ProtGPT2 is a deep unsupervised language model for protein …","doi":"10.1007/s11427-025-3163-6","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000281","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The title/abstract reads like a review of host-microbiome multi-omics methods and benchmarks, not a new model. It mentions LLM-based approaches and ProtGPT2 as examples.","evidence_snippet":"\"methods, benchmarks, and translational applications\"; \"LLM-based approaches\"; \"ProtGPT2\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000281","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This reads as a methods/benchmark review rather than a new model paper. It mentions LLM-based approaches and ProtGPT2, but does not describe the paper's own generative foundation model.","evidence_snippet":"\"methods, benchmarks, and translational applications\"; \"LLM-based approaches\"; \"ProtGPT2 is a deep unsupervised language model\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000282","title":"Generative Sequence Models for Variant Effect Prediction","abstract":"… The same variant may be functionally neutral in one cell type … properties such as gene expression, splicing patterns, … RNA foundation model based on the BERT language model …","doi":"","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000282","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"The record is about generative sequence models for variant effect prediction and mentions an RNA foundation model based on BERT. The abstract does not establish a substantive text-bio bridge beyond sequence-language modeling.","evidence_snippet":"\"Generative Sequence Models for Variant Effect Prediction\"; \"RNA foundation model based on the BERT language model\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000282","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"EC3_not_generative","uncertainty_reason":"foundation_model_evidence_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract points to variant-effect prediction and references a BERT-based RNA foundation model, but it does not clearly describe a genuinely generative architecture. The evidence for foundation-model characteristics is also limited to a brief mention of BERT.","evidence_snippet":"\"variant effect prediction\"; \"RNA foundation model based on the BERT language model\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"EC2_no_substantive_text_bio_bridge","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"primary_model_paper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"no","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000283","title":"Evolutionary trajectory and development landscape of digital life","abstract":"Digital life, as a cutting-edge interdisciplinary field integrating life sciences and digital technologies, has become a pivotal research direction driving the paradigm shift of life science research. This paper aims to systematically trace the evolutionary trajectory of digital life from theoretical hypothesis to engineering implementation, deeply dissect its core technical system and current development status, identify the key bottlenecks restricting its in-depth development, and propose targeted development strategies and future prospects, thereby providing a comprehensive reference for academic research, industrial transformation and the construction of governance systems in this field. The study first combs the multi-stage development process of digital life: starting with von Neumann′s self-replicating automaton theory that laid the theoretical foundation of artificial life, it has experienced the stage of large-scale accumulation of life omics data driven by high-throughput sequencing and super-resolution imaging technologies, the stage of breakthroughs in biological feature decoding relying on synthetic biology and artificial intelligence (AI) models such as AlphaFold, and then entered the stage of practical application of digital twin technology, with the birth of a series of whole-organism models including AI virtual cells, digital organs, intelligent nematode ″Tianbao 1.0″, virtual rats and digital fruit flies. On this basis, the paper elaborates the four core technical pillars supporting the development of digital life: life data acquisition with high-throughput, ultra-high resolution and multi-modal characteristics, life feature decoding empowered by large models such as transformers and graph neural networks, life activity simulation emphasizing wet-dry closed-loop validation and multi-scale model iteration, and the integrated life digital twin system that realizes dynamic coupling between virtual and real entities. It also reveals the core connotation of digital life research in realizing the in-depth integration of spatial, temporal and logical dimensions, and constructing a digital system that conforms to the laws of real life and has controllability. In the research, it is found that digital life is currently in a critical period from concept verification to system engineering, and is facing prominent bottlenecks such as difficulty in integrating multi-source heterogeneous data, weak interpretability of cross-scale models, insufficient understanding of the dynamic causality of life systems, lagging ethical governance systems, and barriers in interdisciplinary collaborative innovation. In response to these problems, this paper holds that the future development of digital life should follow the progressive evolution path of ″division-conjunction-integration″. It is imperative to further deepen the theoretical understanding of the emergent mechanisms of life systems, develop new AI architectures embedded with biophysical constraints to improve the interpretability and predictability of models, establish standardized data platforms and model interfaces to break interdisciplinary barriers and promote open-source collaboration, and construct a comprehensive governance system covering intellectual property protection, ethical review and safety supervision. Only by overcoming the above technical and institutional bottlenecks can we truly realize the comprehensive parsing and reconstruction of the life panorama, promote the mature application of digital life in precision medicine, drug research and development, molecular engineering and other fields, and make digital life an important scientific infrastructure for revealing the mysteries of life and safeguarding human health.","doi":"10.3724/cbls.2026034","year":"2026","venue":"","sources":["google_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000283","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a broad overview of digital life development, bottlenecks, and future prospects rather than a primary model paper. It discusses existing technical pillars and applications instead of proposing a candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically trace the evolutionary trajectory\"; \"current development status\"; \"future prospects\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000283","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"generative_status_unclear","decision_rationale":"This is a broad survey of digital life, its bottlenecks, and future directions rather than a primary model paper. It mentions transformers and graph neural networks only as part of the field overview.","evidence_snippet":"\"systematically trace the evolutionary trajectory\"; \"large models such as transformers and graph neural networks\""},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]

